   "source": [
    "# Heavy-Resonance Search with Cut-Based Selection and Neural Networks\n",
    "\n",
    "**Saqib Umair**  \n",
    "MSc Particle and Nuclear Physics, University of Edinburgh\n",
    "\n",
    "## Aim\n",
    "\n",
    "This project studies a weighted search for a heavy resonance near 1 TeV in a dilepton-plus-fatjet mass spectrum. I compare a transparent cut-based selection with neural-network classifiers and then test how the event selection affects the final mass-spectrum interpretation.\n",
    "\n",
    "The analysis uses one signal sample (`ggH1000`) and three background samples: diboson, Z+jets and top. Event yields are calculated from `FullEventWeight`, not from raw row counts.\n",
    "\n",
    "The analysis is organised around three questions:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. How much do simple kinematic cuts improve signal-to-background separation?\n",
    "2. Can a neural network improve the selection without using the final mass variable?\n",
    "3. What changes when the reconstructed mass itself is included in the classifier?\n",
    "\n",
    "A central result is that the classifier with the highest ROC AUC does **not** produce the best search region. This is a useful example of the difference between classifier performance and analysis-level physics sensitivity.\n"
   ]
  },
