  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4263eadf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 3 Fitting and hypothesis testing\n",
    "## 3.1 Signal-only and background-only fits\n",
    "\n",
    "For the fitting stage, I model the selected signal spectrum with a Gaussian and the selected background spectrum with a smooth polynomial. I use separate fits first because the project asks for the signal and background shapes to be determined independently before constructing the joint model. I also restrict the fit to subranges of the mass spectrum rather than using the full histogram, to focus the fit on the relevant mass region. The Gaussian is a simple approximation for the signal peak, while the polynomial gives a flexible but still smooth description of the falling background shape. I tried different reasonable choices here and kept the simplest model that still gave a sensible visual description of the selected spectra. The background-only fit is good, while the signal-only fit is visually reasonable but not perfect, so I treat the extracted mean and width as an approximate template rather than as an exact description of the signal shape."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "544e3b64",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# I choose a narrower mass range for the fit so I focus on the main region of interest\n",
    "fit_bins_gev = np.linspace(800, 1500, 36)\n",
    "\n",
    "# This function makes a weighted histogram and also returns the weighted bin errors\n",
    "# The bin error is taken from the sum of squared weights\n",
    "def make_weighted_histogram(dataframe, mass_column, bin_edges):\n",
    "    counts = np.histogram(\n",
    "        dataframe[mass_column],\n",
    "        bins=bin_edges,\n",
    "        weights=dataframe[\"FullEventWeight\"]\n",
    "    )[0]\n",
    "\n",
    "    sum_w2 = np.histogram(\n",
    "        dataframe[mass_column],\n",
    "        bins=bin_edges,\n",
    "        weights=dataframe[\"FullEventWeight\"] ** 2\n",
    "    )[0]\n",
    "\n",
    "    errors = np.sqrt(sum_w2)\n",
    "    centres = 0.5 * (bin_edges[:-1] + bin_edges[1:])\n",
    "    return centres, counts, errors\n",
    "\n",
    "# I use a Gaussian for the signal shape\n",
    "def gaussian(x, amplitude, mean, sigma):\n",
    "    return amplitude * np.exp(-0.5 * ((x - mean) / sigma) ** 2)\n",
    "\n",
    "# For the background, I use a simple quadratic polynomial\n",
    "# This is flexible enough for the smooth background shape in this range\n",
    "def background_poly(x, p0, p1, p2):\n",
    "    t = (x - 1000.0) / 300.0\n",
    "    return p0 + p1 * t + p2 * t**2\n",
    "\n",
    "# Weighted chi-square:\n",
    "# observed bin content = sum of weights\n",
    "# bin variance = sum of squared weights\n",
    "def weighted_chi2(observed, predicted, errors):\n",
    "    valid = (errors > 0) & (predicted > 0)\n",
    "    return np.sum(((observed[valid] - predicted[valid]) / errors[valid]) ** 2)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Build weighted histograms for signal, background, and the combined selected sample\n",
    "signal_centres, signal_counts, signal_errors = make_weighted_histogram(\n",
    "    signal_after_cuts, \"reco_zv_mass_gev\", fit_bins_gev\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "background_centres, background_counts, background_errors = make_weighted_histogram(\n",
    "    background_after_cuts, \"reco_zv_mass_gev\", fit_bins_gev\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "joint_after_cuts = pd.concat([signal_after_cuts, background_after_cuts], ignore_index=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "joint_centres, joint_counts, joint_errors = make_weighted_histogram(\n",
    "    joint_after_cuts, \"reco_zv_mass_gev\", fit_bins_gev\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# I fit the signal only in a region around the peak\n",
    "signal_mask = (\n",
    "    (signal_centres >= 920) &\n",
    "    (signal_centres <= 1080) &\n",
    "    (signal_errors > 0)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# For the background and joint fits, I use a wider range\n",
    "background_mask = (\n",
    "    (background_centres >= 850) &\n",
    "    (background_centres <= 1450) &\n",
    "    (background_errors > 0)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "joint_mask = (\n",
    "    (joint_centres >= 850) &\n",
    "    (joint_centres <= 1450) &\n",
    "    (joint_errors > 0)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# First fit the signal-only histogram\n",
    "# This gives the mean and width that I later fix in the H1 fit\n",
    "def signal_chi2(params):\n",
    "    amplitude, mean, sigma = params\n",
    "    predicted = gaussian(signal_centres[signal_mask], amplitude, mean, sigma)\n",
    "    return weighted_chi2(signal_counts[signal_mask], predicted, signal_errors[signal_mask])\n",
    "\n",
    "signal_guess = [signal_counts[signal_mask].max(), 1000.0, 80.0]\n",
    "signal_bounds = [(0, None), (900, 1100), (20, 200)]\n",
    "\n",
    "signal_fit = minimize(signal_chi2, signal_guess, bounds=signal_bounds)\n",
    "signal_amplitude_fit, signal_mean_fit, signal_sigma_fit = signal_fit.x\n",
    "signal_chi2_min = signal_fit.fun\n",
    "signal_ndof = signal_mask.sum() - 3\n",
    "\n",
    "# Next fit the background-only spectrum\n",
    "def background_chi2(params):\n",
    "    p0, p1, p2 = params\n",
    "    predicted = background_poly(background_centres[background_mask], p0, p1, p2)\n",
    "    if np.any(predicted <= 0):\n",
    "        return 1e30\n",
    "    return weighted_chi2(background_counts[background_mask], predicted, background_errors[background_mask])\n",
    "\n",
    "background_guess = [\n",
    "    np.median(background_counts[background_mask]),\n",
    "    -0.5 * np.median(background_counts[background_mask]),\n",
    "    0.1 * np.median(background_counts[background_mask]),\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "background_fit = minimize(background_chi2, background_guess)\n",
    "background_p0_fit, background_p1_fit, background_p2_fit = background_fit.x\n",
    "background_chi2_min = background_fit.fun\n",
    "background_ndof = background_mask.sum() - 3\n",
    "\n",
    "# H0 means background only\n",
    "# Here I fit the combined selected spectrum with no signal term\n",
    "def h0_chi2(params):\n",
    "    p0, p1, p2 = params\n",
    "    predicted = background_poly(joint_centres[joint_mask], p0, p1, p2)\n",
    "    if np.any(predicted <= 0):\n",
    "        return 1e30\n",
    "    return weighted_chi2(joint_counts[joint_mask], predicted, joint_errors[joint_mask])\n",
    "\n",
    "h0_guess = [background_p0_fit, background_p1_fit, background_p2_fit]\n",
    "h0_fit = minimize(h0_chi2, h0_guess)\n",
    "h0_p0_fit, h0_p1_fit, h0_p2_fit = h0_fit.x\n",
    "h0_chi2_min = h0_fit.fun\n",
    "h0_ndof = joint_mask.sum() - 3\n",
    "\n",
    "# H1 means signal plus background\n",
    "# The signal mean and width are fixed from the signal-only fit\n",
    "# Only the signal amplitude is free here\n",
    "def h1_chi2(params):\n",
    "    signal_amp, p0, p1, p2 = params\n",
    "    predicted = (\n",
    "        gaussian(joint_centres[joint_mask], signal_amp, signal_mean_fit, signal_sigma_fit)\n",
    "        + background_poly(joint_centres[joint_mask], p0, p1, p2)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    if np.any(predicted <= 0):\n",
    "        return 1e30\n",
    "    return weighted_chi2(joint_counts[joint_mask], predicted, joint_errors[joint_mask])\n",
    "\n",
    "h1_guess = [signal_amplitude_fit, background_p0_fit, background_p1_fit, background_p2_fit]\n",
    "h1_bounds = [(0, None), (None, None), (None, None), (None, None)]\n",
    "\n",
    "h1_fit = minimize(h1_chi2, h1_guess, bounds=h1_bounds)\n",
    "h1_signal_amp_fit, h1_p0_fit, h1_p1_fit, h1_p2_fit = h1_fit.x\n",
    "h1_chi2_min = h1_fit.fun\n",
    "h1_ndof = joint_mask.sum() - 4\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compare H0 and H1 using Delta chi-square\n",
    "# With Wilks' theorem, this can be turned into a p-value and Z-value\n",
    "delta_chi2 = h0_chi2_min - h1_chi2_min\n",
    "p_value = chi2.sf(delta_chi2, 1)\n",
    "z_value = norm.isf(p_value)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Print all fit results clearly\n",
    "print(\"Signal-only fit\")\n",
    "print(f\"mean  = {signal_mean_fit:.3f} GeV\")\n",
    "print(f\"sigma = {signal_sigma_fit:.3f} GeV\")\n",
    "print(f\"chi2 / ndof = {signal_chi2_min:.3f} / {signal_ndof}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nBackground-only fit\")\n",
    "print(f\"p0 = {background_p0_fit:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"p1 = {background_p1_fit:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"p2 = {background_p2_fit:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"chi2 / ndof = {background_chi2_min:.3f} / {background_ndof}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nH0 fit\")\n",
    "print(f\"chi2 / ndof = {h0_chi2_min:.3f} / {h0_ndof}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nH1 fit\")\n",
    "print(f\"signal amplitude = {h1_signal_amp_fit:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"chi2 / ndof = {h1_chi2_min:.3f} / {h1_ndof}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nHypothesis test\")\n",
    "print(f\"Delta chi2 = {delta_chi2:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"p-value    = {p_value:.6g}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Z-value    = {z_value:.3f} sigma\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4a2a91d6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### H0 vs H1 hypothesis test after cuts\n",
    "\n",
    "After the cuts, I test two nested models on the selected `reco_zv_mass` spectrum. In the background-only hypothesis, $H_0$, the data are described using only the background shape. In the signal-plus-background hypothesis, $H_1$, I add a Gaussian signal term. The Gaussian mean and width are fixed from the signal-only fit, while the signal normalisation is left free, and the background parameters are still allowed to vary. The Gaussian mean and width are fixed from the signal-only fit, while the signal normalisation is allowed to vary.\n",
    "\n",
    "Since the events are weighted, I do not use a simple counting-based $\\chi^2$. Instead, I use the modified binned $\\chi^2$ form in which the content of each bin is the sum of event weights and the variance is taken as the sum of squared weights in that bin. This makes the test consistent with the weighted nature of the dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the cut-based selection, the fit gives\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\chi^2 / \\mathrm{ndof} = 80.664/28\n",
    "$$\n",
    "for $H_0$, and\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\chi^2 / \\mathrm{ndof} = 28.726/27\n",
    "$$\n",
    "for $H_1$.\n",
    "So, once the signal component is included, the fit improves significantly.\n",
    "\n",
    "The difference between the two fits is\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\Delta \\chi^2 = 51.938,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "which corresponds to a p-value of\n",
    "$$\n",
    "5.73 \\times 10^{-13}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "and a significance of\n",
    "$$\n",
    "7.11\\,\\sigma.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The main point is that, after the cuts, the selected mass spectrum is described much better by a model that includes a peak near $1~\\mathrm{TeV}$ than by a background-only model. At the same time, the p-value should be interpreted carefully: it is not the probability that $H_0$ is true. Instead, it is the probability of obtaining data at least this extreme if $H_0$ were true."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4ba42a02",
